WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:09.915
In many games, when the camera slides past a pillar or a character, you can catch a faint smear trailing along its edge. A ghost of where it used to be.

2
00:00:10.265 --> 00:00:19.353
Here is our test scene. A metro platform, with square pillars and a thin railing. Nothing in it moves except the camera, which slides sideways.

3
00:00:19.703 --> 00:00:25.932
So why would a perfectly still pillar leave a ghost? The answer is a question the renderer forgot to ask.

4
00:00:27.132 --> 00:00:36.092
Modern renderers save work by reusing the previous frame. Blending old pixels with new ones smooths out jagged edges and noise.

5
00:00:36.442 --> 00:00:43.077
The final colour is a weight w times the history, plus one minus w times the current shading.

6
00:00:43.427 --> 00:00:52.771
Here w is zero point nine. Ninety percent of each pixel comes from the past. That is powerful, as long as the past is the right past.

7
00:00:53.971 --> 00:01:00.477
But the camera moved. So for each pixel, the renderer asks where this point was on screen one frame ago.

8
00:01:00.827 --> 00:01:08.742
It knows the depth of the pixel and both camera positions, so it can trace the point back exactly. This is called reprojection.

9
00:01:09.092 --> 00:01:13.956
In our scene, the nearest pillar slides about fourteen and a half pixels per frame.

10
00:01:15.156 --> 00:01:21.599
Now watch the edge of a pillar. As the camera slides, a strip of the wall behind it comes into view.

11
00:01:21.949 --> 00:01:28.903
Those pixels show the far wall now. But one frame ago, that same spot on screen was covered by the pillar.

12
00:01:29.253 --> 00:01:37.083
So the history that comes back is pillar, not wall. Wrong surface. Blend ninety percent of that, and you paint a ghost of the pillar.

13
00:01:38.283 --> 00:01:47.797
The classic safety check is a colour clamp. Look at the three by three neighbourhood of the current pixel, and squeeze the history colour inside their range.

14
00:01:48.147 --> 00:01:51.134
It asks, does this old colour look plausible here?

15
00:01:51.484 --> 00:02:01.553
But right at a pillar edge, the neighbourhood holds pillar, floor and wall all at once. Pillar colour fits inside that range. The clamp lets the ghost through.

16
00:02:02.753 --> 00:02:07.809
So ask a different question. Not, does it look right, but, is it the same surface?

17
00:02:08.159 --> 00:02:14.175
Compare the depth stored in history with the depth this point should have had. Take their relative difference.

18
00:02:14.525 --> 00:02:23.123
If it is bigger than tau, two percent, the old pixel belongs to something else. Set the weight to zero and trust the current frame alone.

19
00:02:23.473 --> 00:02:34.438
Here is the aha. Colour asks whether the past looks right. Depth asks whether the past is even the same thing. At an edge, only the second question has the right answer.

20
00:02:35.638 --> 00:02:44.427
Here is the measured failure mask. Red marks pixels whose history came from the wrong surface. Blue marks history that slid off screen.

21
00:02:44.777 --> 00:02:58.409
The red hugs every pillar silhouette and the railing. In total, two point four nine percent of the frame is rejected. One point four two percent by depth, one point zero seven percent off screen.

22
00:02:58.759 --> 00:03:10.215
Around those edges, the error of the colour clamp alone is zero point one four zero. With the depth test, it drops to zero point zero five four, well under half.

23
00:03:11.415 --> 00:03:23.341
What if we change tau? At half a percent, two percent and eight percent, the rejected fraction barely moves, from two point five zero three to two point four six four percent.

24
00:03:23.691 --> 00:03:32.245
That is because depth edges are cliffs, not slopes. Wrong-surface history is wildly wrong, so any sensible threshold catches it.

25
00:03:32.595 --> 00:03:39.763
And with a still camera, nothing is rejected at all. Both methods give the same image, exactly as they should.

26
00:03:40.963 --> 00:03:50.158
What this does not prove. In the final pixels, the difference is subtle. The ghost lives in a thin strip, and the numbers show it better than the eye.

27
00:03:50.508 --> 00:03:59.660
Here each pixel reuses one frame, not a long trail of frames. And the thin railing still lags a little, because it is thinner than a pixel.

28
00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:05.749
It is an offline software render, not a game benchmark. All thirty seven checks passed.

29
00:04:06.949 --> 00:04:13.861
The takeaway. When you reuse the past, checking that it looks right is not enough. Check that it is the same surface.

30
00:04:14.211 --> 00:04:22.488
Colour clamps catch implausible colours. Depth tests catch the wrong object. Temporal techniques in real engines use both.

31
00:04:22.838 --> 00:04:31.670
For further reading, see Nehab and colleagues on reverse reprojection caching, and Brian Karis on high quality temporal supersampling.
